%[text] # Generate C++ Class Interface from Multi-Instance Export-Function Model
%[text] This example shows you how to configure an export-function model that calls the same independent algorithm multiple times to generate reentrant, multi-instance C++ class code. When you configure the code interface packaging for a model to produce a C++ class interface, the model algorithm and associated data are generated as an encapsulated C++ class. The entry-point step function for the model is a method of the class, and it operates directly on data members of the class.
%[text] After reviewing the model and configuration settings, generate code, and then review the generated code.
%[text] ## **Open the Model and Review Configuration**
%[text] Open the export-function model `CppMultiInstance`. The model contains two Model blocks named `CppEventCounterA` and `CppEventCounterB` that each reference the model named `CppEventCounter`.
model='CppMultiInstance';
open_system(model);
%[text] ![Image of CppMultiInstance contains two instances of model CppEventCounter.](text:image:9086)
%[text] You can verify that the model is designated as an export-function model by using the Property Inspector. Open the Property Inspector, select the **Execution** tab, and verify **Set execution domain** is selected and **Domain** is set to `Export function`.
%[text] ![The model domain is specified as Export function under the Execution tab of the Property Inspector.](text:image:0512)
%[text] ### **Examine Relevant Model Configuration Parameter Settings**
%[text] Verify the configuration settings for the top model `CppMultiInstance.`
%[text] 1\. Open the Embedded Coder app.
%[text] 2\. Open the Model Configuration Parameters dialog box.
%[text] 3\. Open the **Solver **pane and specify the configuration settings required for generating code from export-function models.
%[text] - Set **Type** to `Fixed-step`.
%[text] - Set **Solver** to `auto (Automatic solver selection)` or `discrete (no continuous states)`.
%[text] - Set **Periodic sample time constraint** to `Unconstrained`. \
%[text] 4\. Open the **Code Generation** pane. Under **Target selection**, confirm that **Language** is set to `C++`. 
%[text] 5\. Open the **Code Generation \> Interface** pane.
%[text] - **Code interface packaging** is set to `C++ class`. This parameter setting is required to instruct the code generator to produce a C++ class interface.
%[text] - Setting **Code interface packaging** to `C++ class` enables you to set the parameter **Multi-instance code error diagnostic**. For this example, this parameter is set to `Error`, indicating that the code generator aborts if the model violates requirements for generating multi-instance code.
%[text] - **Remove error status field in real-time model data structure** is selected. Selecting this parameter specifies whether to omit the error status field from the generated real-time model data structure `rtModel`, which results in more compact generated code if the error status is not needed.
%[text] - **Include model types in model class** is selected. Selecting this parameter specifies to nest `struct` types in the body of the model class definition.
%[text] - Under **Advanced parameters**, the parameter **Use dynamic memory allocation for model block instantiation** is not selected. This parameter is cleared by default, which results in a parent model maintaining information about its referenced models, including its direct and indirect children. If you select this parameter, the code generator uses dynamic memory allocation to instantiate objects for referenced models using the `new` operator. \
%[text] For additional information about workflows and other requirements for export-function modeling, see [Export-Function Models Overview](file:matlab:open('docid:simulink_ug#bt170no')).
%[text] The configuration parameter settings for the referenced model `CppEventCounter` are the same as for the top-model `CppMultiInstance.`
%[text] ### Configure Data Mappings
%[text] When generating code for this model, the class definition of the model encapsulates the inports, outports, model parameters, signals, states, and internal data. You can specify the visibility and access method for the data members by using the Code Mappings editor. See [Code Mappings](docid:ecoder_ref#mw_9e0788eb-fc60-4606-9ffe-a273092602d5-2). 
%[text] For this example: 
%[text] - Outports are defined as public members, accessed by an inlined, structure-based method.
%[text] - Model parameter arguments are private members, accessed by an aggregate structure-based get and set method.
%[text] - Model parameters, signals, states, and internal data are public members. \
%[text] To view the code mappings, in the Embedded Coder toolbar, select **Code Interface** \> **Code Mappings**. Data mappings are located under the **Data** tab.
%[text] ![](text:image:42cd)
%[text] For detailed information about configuring data members, see [Interactively Configure C++ Interface](docid:ecoder_ug#mw_5b3f3578-dd1d-4ee1-9e55-1f9093d93c7f) and [Configuring Parameters for C++ Class Code Generation](docid:ecoder_ug#mw_b241c378-35d9-4c0d-85cd-500478927eec).
%[text] ### Configure Entry-Point Method Mappings
%[text] The model class also encapsulates entry-point methods in the generated code. You can specify the names of entry-point methods by using the Code Mapping editor. Method name mappings are located under the **Functions** tab.
%[text] In the generated code, each root-level function-call Inport block generates an entry-point method. For this example: 
%[text] - The exported function on the Input block **eventA** is renamed to **inputEventA**.
%[text] - The exported function on the Input block **eventB** is renamed to **inputEventB**.
%[text] - **Initialize** and **Terminate** methods keep their default names. \
%[text] ![](text:image:3855)
%%
%[text] ## **Review the Referenced Model**
%[text] Double-click `CppEventCounter` to open the referenced model. The referenced model includes a Function-Call Subsystem that contains the algorithm triggered by input method calls.
%[text] ![The image shows the model CppEventCounter containing a Function-Call Subsystem and displays the contents of the Function-Call Subsystem, which consists of an event counter algorithm.](text:image:41c3)
%[text] The model functions as an event counter and consists of a Trigger block (`function`), a Bias block (`Index`), a Unit Delay block, and an Output port. The Trigger block has **Trigger type** set to `function-call,` which causes the model to execute when the control port receives a function-call event.
%[text] The configuration settings and data mappings for the referenced model `CppEventCounter` are the same as for the top-model `CppMultiInstance`. The algorithm step method is named `counterStep`.
%%
%[text] ## **Generate and Review Code**
slbuild(model);
%[text] The code generator places model-specific generated files for the model `CppMultiInstance` in the folder `CppMultiInstance_ert_rtw and` shared files in folder `slprj`. Generated files for the reference model `CppEventCounter` are located in the shared folder in `slprj/ert/CppEventCounter.`
%[text] The code generator creates a model-specific class for each model. Objects of the model class for each referenced model are created in the top model definition, where each object is stored as a data member of the parent model class.
%%
%[text] ## **Inspect Generated Class Definition for Multi-Instance Referenced Model**
%[text] The code generator places model class definitions in the *`model`*`.h` file for the referenced model. For this example, the code generator places the class definition for `CppEventCounter` in the file `slprj/ert/CppEventCounter/CppEventCounter.h`.
%[text] Review the class definition for `CppEventCounter.`
%[text] - The block state for the unit delay is declared in public structure `DW_CppEventCounter_T`.
%[text] - The event export function is declared as the public method `counterStep`, as configured in the code mappings for the referenced model. \
%[text] ```
%[text] class CppEventCounter final
%[text] {
%[text]  public:
%[text]   // Block states (default storage) for model 'CppEventCounter'
%[text]   struct DW_CppEventCounter_T {
%[text]     real_T UnitDelay_DSTATE;           // '<S1>/Unit Delay'
%[text]   };
%[text] 
%[text]   ...
%[text] 
%[text]   // model step function
%[text]   void counterStep(real_T *rty_Outport);
%[text] 
%[text]   // Block states
%[text]   DW_CppEventCounter_T CppEventCounter_DW;
%[text] 
%[text]   // Constructor
%[text]   CppEventCounter();
%[text] 
%[text]   // Destructor
%[text]   ~CppEventCounter();
%[text] };
%[text] ```
%[text] The class name defaults to the model name. You can customize the class name and namespace by selecting **Code Interface** \> **Class Name & Namespace**. For more information, see [Interactively Configure C++ Interface](docid:ecoder_ug#mw_5b3f3578-dd1d-4ee1-9e55-1f9093d93c7f).
%%
%[text] ## **Inspect Generated Algorithm Code for Multi-Instance Referenced Model**
%[text] The code generator places the algorithm code in the *`model`*`.cpp` file for the referenced model. For this example, the code generator places code for model `CppEventCounter` in the file `slprj/ert/CppEventCounter/CppEventCounter.cpp`.
%[text] ```
%[text] #include "CppEventCounter.h"
%[text] #include "rtwtypes.h"
%[text] 
%[text] void CppEventCounter::counterStep(real_T *rty_Out1)
%[text] {
%[text]   *rty_Out1 = CppEventCounter_DW.UnitDelay_DSTATE;
%[text] 
%[text]   CppEventCounter_DW.UnitDelay_DSTATE = *rty_Out1 + 1.0;
%[text] }
%[text] ...
%[text] ```
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## **Inspect Generated Class Definition for Top Model**
%[text] For the top model, the code generator defines the model class `CppMultiInstance` in the file `CppMultiInstance_ert_rtw/CppMultiInstance.h`. 
%[text] Review the class definition for `CppMultiInstance.`
%[text] - Outputs are declared in public structure `ExtY_CppMultiInstance_T` and can be accessed by using the inlined method `getExternalOutputs`.
%[text] - Entry-point methods `initialize`, `inputEventA`, `inputEventB`, and `terminate` are declared as public methods.
%[text] - Instances of type `CppEventCounter` are declared as private members of `CppMultiInstance.` \
%[text] ```
%[text] class CppMultiInstance final
%[text] {
%[text]  public:
%[text]   // External outputs (root outports fed by signals with default storage)
%[text]   struct ExtY_CppMultiInstance_T {
%[text]     real_T countA;                     // '<Root>/countA'
%[text]     real_T countB;                     // '<Root>/countB'
%[text]   };
%[text] 
%[text]   ...
%[text] 
%[text]   // External outputs
%[text]   ExtY_CppMultiInstance_T CppMultiInstance_Y;
%[text] 
%[text]   // Root outports get method
%[text]   const ExtY_CppMultiInstance_T &getExternalOutputs() const
%[text]   {
%[text]     return CppMultiInstance_Y;
%[text]   }
%[text]   
%[text]   // model initialize function
%[text]   void initialize();
%[text] 
%[text]   // model step function
%[text]   void inputEventA();
%[text] 
%[text]   // model step function
%[text]   void inputEventB();
%[text] 
%[text]   // model terminate function
%[text]   static void terminate();
%[text] 
%[text]   ...
%[text] 
%[text]  private:
%[text]   // model instance variable for '<Root>/CppEventCounterA'
%[text]   CppEventCounter CppEventCounterAMDLOBJ1;
%[text] 
%[text]   // model instance variable for '<Root>/CppEventCounterB'
%[text]   CppEventCounter CppEventCounterBMDLOBJ2;
%[text]   
%[text]   ...
%[text] }
%[text] ```
%[text] Code generation controls the naming of class objects based on the model construction. Object names, such as `CppEventCounterAMDLOBJ1` and `CppEventCounterBMDLOBJ2` in this example, cannot be customized. 
%%
%[text] ## **Inspect Generated Entry-Point Methods for Top Model**
%[text] The code generator places the entry-point method code for the top model `CppMultiInstance` in the file `CppMultiInstance_ert_rtw/CppMultiInstance.cpp`.
%[text] In the generated code, each root-level function-call Inport block calls an instance-specific entry-point method.
%[text] ```
%[text] #include "CppMultiInstance.h"
%[text] #include "CppEventCounter.h"
%[text] 
%[text] // Model step function
%[text] void CppMultiInstance::inputEventA()
%[text] {
%[text]   CppEventCounterAMDLOBJ1.counterStep(&CppMultiInstance_Y.countA);
%[text] }
%[text] 
%[text] void CppMultiInstance::inputEventB()
%[text] {
%[text]   CppEventCounterBMDLOBJ2.counterStep(&CppMultiInstance_Y.countB);
%[text] }
%[text] 
%[text] // Model initialize function
%[text] void CppMultiInstance::initialize() 
%[text] {
%[text] ...
%[text] }
%[text] ```
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## **Close Models**
%[text] Close the model and the code generation report.
bdclose(model)
